<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1993 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">No Motherland Without You</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ko">당신이 없으면 조국도 없다</bdi> / <bdi lang="ko-Latn">Dangsini eopseumyeon jogukdo eopda</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A North Korean song of 1993 in praise of the leader Kim Jong Il, often played on the radio and from street loudspeakers in Pyongyang.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ri Jong-o</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Hwang Jin-yong</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Democratic People's Republic of Korea (North Korea)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>North Korea</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1993</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Ri Jong-o and music by Hwang Jin-yong, 1993)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/no-motherland-without-you">No Motherland Without You</a>&quot;, also called &quot;Ode to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-jong-il">Kim Jong Il</a>&quot;, is a North Korean song about the country's second supreme leader, Kim Jong Il. It was first released in 1993, with music by Hwang Jin-yong and words by Ri Jong-o. Written especially for Kim Jong Il, it is regarded as his signature song, and it extols his proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and virtues and the people's loyalty to him.</p><p>The song is popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and is often broadcast on the radio and from loudspeakers in the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>. Where the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-general-kim-il-sung">Song of General Kim Il Sung</a>&quot; was sung at the beginning of public gatherings, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/no-motherland-without-you">No Motherland Without You</a>&quot; was often sung at the end. From at least 1994, before the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-general-kim-jong-il">Song of General Kim Jong Il</a>&quot; was written, state television opened each day's broadcasts with it.</p><p>It exists in two versions. The civilian words call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-jong-il">Kim Jong Il</a> &quot;Comrade&quot;, while the military version calls him &quot;General&quot;, as he had been Supreme Commander of the Korean People's Army since 1991.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of loyalty and devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-jong-il">Kim Jong Il</a>; solemn and hymn-like.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="7258363"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/Pochonbo-electronic-ensemble-no-motherland-without-you-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
